WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.220
Exposure is not one brightness dial.

2
00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:08.057
It is the combined result of aperture, shutter
speed, and ISO.

3
00:00:08.057 --> 00:00:13.641
If one setting admits or records less light,
another may need to compensate.

4
00:00:13.641 --> 00:00:19.605
The useful question is not only whether the
image is bright enough, but what visual

5
00:00:19.605 --> 00:00:21.195
trade-off each setting creates.

6
00:00:21.314 --> 00:00:26.454
A wider aperture admits more light and can
produce shallower depth of field.

7
00:00:26.454 --> 00:00:31.630
A narrower aperture admits less light and can
keep more of the scene in focus.

8
00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:37.473
A fast shutter can freeze motion; a slow
shutter records motion across time.

9
00:00:37.473 --> 00:00:41.505
These are creative decisions as well as
exposure decisions.

10
00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:47.399
When depth of field and motion already
determine aperture and shutter speed, ISO can

11
00:00:47.399 --> 00:00:49.481
help reach a usable brightness.

12
00:00:49.481 --> 00:00:53.721
Raising it can also make noise more visible
and reduce image quality.

13
00:00:53.721 --> 00:00:59.738
Start from the look the photograph needs, then
balance the three controls instead of treating

14
00:00:59.738 --> 00:01:02.145
any one setting as automatically correct.
